<DocsLayout
  title="First Project"
  description="Open your first workspace and learn the core Axon workflow."
>
  <h1>First Project</h1>

  <p>
    The fastest way to understand Axon is to open a real project folder. Axon
    treats the workspace folder as the base for file operations, terminal
    sessions, Git state, search, and language-server startup.
  </p>

  <h2>Open a workspace</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Launch Axon.</li>
    <li>Use the workspace button in the title bar to choose a folder.</li>
    <li>Wait for the sidebar to load the folder tree.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>Create and edit files</h2>
  <p>
    Use the sidebar context menu to create files and folders. New entries appear
    in the tree, inherit Git decorations from their parent folders, and can be
    opened immediately in the editor pane.
  </p>

  <h2>Use terminal and Git</h2>
  <p>
    Open the terminal from the status bar when you need project commands. Open
    Source Control when you want changed files, diffs, and history without
    leaving the editor.
  </p>

  <h2>Set up language intelligence</h2>
  <p>
    Axon starts language servers based on the files in your workspace. Python
    projects should select a virtual environment when imports live outside the
    system Python installation.
  </p>
</DocsLayout>
